% ============================================================================
% cloud-types — each common cloud and the altitude level (étage) it belongs to
% (adj-facts-stdlib, EARTH-SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first things a child learns to read in the sky: clouds live at
% different HEIGHTS, and forecasters sort them into three layers — a HIGH deck,
% a MIDDLE deck, and a LOW deck (the French word for these decks is "étage").
% Which layer a cloud belongs to is a plain fact you can look up. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? cloud_altitude(cirrus, $Level)      % high  (cirrus is a high cloud)
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `cloud_altitude(cloud, altitude)` carrying the NOAA / National
% Weather Service citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the level WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls. The relation binds BOTH ways: forward `? cloud_altitude(stratus,
% $L)` gives low, and reverse `? cloud_altitude($C, middle)` gives altostratus.
% Both atoms — the cloud name and its level — are lowercase.
%
% The seven clouds, and the deck each one sits in, as a truth table:
%
%     cloud         altitude   why (the level word the NWS page uses)
%     ------------  ---------  ---------------------------------------------
%     cirrus        high       "high clouds ... cirrus"        (above ~20,000 ft)
%     cirrostratus  high       "high clouds ... cirrostratus"
%     cirrocumulus  high       "high clouds ... cirrocumulus"
%     altostratus   middle     "mid-level clouds ... altostratus" (6,500–20,000 ft)
%     altocumulus   middle     "mid-level clouds ... altocumulus"
%     stratus       low        "low clouds include stratus"   (below 6,500 ft)
%     cumulus       low        "low clouds include ... cumulus"
%
% The page uses the level words HIGH, MIDDLE ("the middle level of the
% troposphere"), and LOW, so those exact words are the values shipped here.
%
% Honest abstention: only clouds the NWS page actually assigns to a level are
% rows. A word the page does not classify — fog, a contrail, or a made-up
% "rainbow" — is NOT a row, so the engine stays silent rather than invent a
% level. That honest-miss property is what makes the table safe to trust.
%
% Provenance: the seven cloud → level groupings come from NOAA's National
% Weather Service (Louisville, KY / LMK office) "Cloud Classification and
% Characteristics" page. The page carries THREE separate "main types"
% sentences, one per deck, and each occurs exactly once in the page's text:
% the high deck (cirrus, cirrostratus, cirrocumulus), the mid-level deck
% (altostratus, altocumulus) and the low deck (stratus, cumulus). They are NOT
% adjacent -- 1443 characters of other prose and photo captions sit between
% the first and second, and 1446 between the second and third, measured with
% U+00A0 folded to a space; collapsing ONLY ASCII whitespace, which is the
% rule the counts below are measured under, the same gaps are 1704 and
% 1483 -- so the
% three deck sentences are now carried one per row, each by the rows whose
% cloud it names. Each of the three is a real contiguous span; the
% string that welded them into one was not.
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN DECK SENTENCE (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The
% envelope used to be the HIGH-deck sentence and the other two decks were
% table-level `cites`, so every answer carried all three sentences and four
% rows -- altostratus, altocumulus, stratus, cumulus -- had a PRIMARY source
% that named a different cloud. Each row now carries the one deck sentence
% that names its own cloud, and the envelope is the page's classification
% sentence, which names no cloud and no level:
%
%     "Clouds are classified according to their height above and
%      appearance (texture) from the ground."
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same
% host returns a real 404 that holds none of these sentences), with inline
% tags removed without a space and only ASCII whitespace collapsed: each of
% the three deck sentences occurs exactly once, inside one `<p>`, with no
% `<head>` copy; the envelope likewise occurs exactly once inside one `<p>`.
% Every row's page is the envelope's, so no row restates a `locator` line or
% `trust` (ADJ-TABLES section 4).
%
% Two things that look like defects and are not. FIRST, the mid-level sentence
% reads "The two main TYPE of mid-level clouds", singular, and that is the
% PAGE'S OWN WORDING -- the raw HTML reads `The two main type of <em>mid-level
% clouds</em>`. Reproducing the slip is faithful; correcting it would be the
% defect. SECOND, a tag-stripping extractor that replaces every tag with a
% break scores all three sentences ZERO against this page, because each cloud
% name is wrapped in `<font color>`/`<span style>` for colour -- so
% `<span>cirrus</span>, ` becomes `cirrus , `, with a space before the comma
% that no reader ever sees and that the document's text content never
% contains. Extracting TEXT NODES and breaking only on BLOCK elements, which
% is what a browser does, finds each sentence exactly once and byte-exact. The
% zero was the instrument, not the artifact.
%
% `trust authoritative` -- weather.gov (NOAA/NWS) is a U.S. government (.gov)
% primary source. Nothing here is asserted from memory.
%
% Distinct from the sibling `meteorology/cloud-type.adj`, which
% answers a completely different question (what WEATHER does a cloud's
% presence indicate?) from the SAME NWS page's separate weather-indication
% sentences, not the altitude-deck sentences this table uses (three of them
% since installment 4k split the weld) — the two tables
% do not overlap even though `altocumulus` happens to be named (as an
% abstention target, for an unrelated reason) in both files. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table cloud_altitude {
    columns cloud, altitude

    row (cirrus, high) {
        source "The three main types of high clouds are cirrus, cirrostratus, and cirrocumulus."
    }
    row (cirrostratus, high) {
        source "The three main types of high clouds are cirrus, cirrostratus, and cirrocumulus."
    }
    row (cirrocumulus, high) {
        source "The three main types of high clouds are cirrus, cirrostratus, and cirrocumulus."
    }
    row (altostratus, middle) {
        source "The two main type of mid-level clouds are altostratus and altocumulus."
    }
    row (altocumulus, middle) {
        source "The two main type of mid-level clouds are altostratus and altocumulus."
    }
    row (stratus, low) {
        source "The two main types of low clouds include stratus, which develop horizontally, and cumulus, which develop vertically."
    }
    row (cumulus, low) {
        source "The two main types of low clouds include stratus, which develop horizontally, and cumulus, which develop vertically."
    }

    source "Clouds are classified according to their height above and appearance (texture) from the ground."
    locator "https://www.weather.gov/lmk/cloud_classification"
    trust authoritative
}
